from international_completion_helpers import add,save
add(1978,1,'Number Theory',4.5,'Separate the modulus into eight and 125, then determine the exact multiplicative order using a binomial congruence rather than testing exponents.',r'For positive integers $m<n$, the last three decimal digits of $1978^m$ and $1978^n$ coincide. Find the pair minimising $m+n$.',r'''Put $d=n-m$. Divisibility by eight of $1978^m(1978^d-1)$ forces $m\ge3$, since the second factor is odd. Modulo 125, the base is 103. Modulo five its order is four, so $4\mid d$. Directly $103^2\equiv109$ and $103^4\equiv6\pmod{125}$. Writing $d=4t$, the binomial theorem gives $6^t\equiv1+5t+25t(t-1)/2\pmod{125}$. First modulo 25 this forces $5\mid t$. For $t=5s$, the quadratic term vanishes modulo 125 and $6^t\equiv1+25s$, so $5\mid s$. Therefore $100\mid d$, and $d=100$ works. The least pair is $(m,n)=(3,103)$, with sum 106.''',['Multiplicative order','Modular arithmetic','Binomial coefficients'])
add(1978,2,'Geometry',7,'A vector identity gives the containing sphere, but proving the entire sphere is attained requires choosing an orthonormal basis on which a quadratic form has equal diagonal values.',r'Fix a point $P$ inside a sphere of radius $R$ and centre $O$. Three mutually perpendicular rays from $P$ meet the sphere at $U,V,W$. Complete the rectangular box with edges $PU,PV,PW$, and let $Q$ be opposite $P$. Determine the full locus of $Q$.',r'''The locus is the sphere centred at $O$ with radius $\sqrt{3R^2-2OP^2}$.

Take $O$ as origin, write $p=\overrightarrow{OP}$, and put $c=R^2-|p|^2>0$. Let the three unit directions be $e_i$ and edge lengths $t_i$. The sphere equations give $t_i^2+2t_i(p\cdot e_i)=c$. For $q=p+\sum t_ie_i$, adding gives $|q|^2=|p|^2+3c$.

Conversely take any $q$ with that norm. Define $H(e)=(q\cdot e)^2-(p\cdot e)^2$ on unit vectors. Its values on every orthonormal triple sum to $3c$. Start with any such triple; its minimum and maximum values bracket $c$, so continuity along a spherical path supplies a unit $e_1$ with $H(e_1)=c$. On the perpendicular plane, the values at perpendicular unit vectors sum to $2c$; continuity around its unit circle supplies $e_2$ with value $c$, and its perpendicular $e_3$ then also has value $c$.

Orient each $e_i$ so that $t_i=(q-p)\cdot e_i>0$. This is possible because $H(e_i)=c>0$ rules out $t_i=0$. The equation $H(e_i)=c$ becomes $t_i^2+2t_i(p\cdot e_i)=c$, so $p+t_ie_i$ lies on the original sphere. These rays yield exactly $q$, proving the converse.''',['Solid geometry','Coordinates','Vectors','Locus','Continuity'])
add(1978,3,'Algebra',6,'Complementarity yields two self-indexing recursion rules; use a carefully chosen chain of known values to reach the requested index.',r'Increasing positive-integer sequences $f(1),f(2),\ldots$ and $g(1),g(2),\ldots$ partition the positive integers and satisfy $g(n)=f(f(n))+1$. Determine $f(240)$.',r'''We have $f(1)=1$, since $g(1)\ge2$. No two consecutive integers lie in the $g$ sequence: the predecessor of $g(n)$ is $f(f(n))$.

If $f(n)=k$, count the integers up to $g(n)=f(k)+1$. Exactly $k$ belong to the $f$ sequence and exactly $n$ to the $g$ sequence. Hence $f(k)=n+k-1$. The next integer after $g(n)$ belongs to the $f$ sequence, giving $f(k+1)=n+k+1$.

Starting with $f(1)=1$, these two rules successively give
$$f(2)=3,\ f(3)=4,\ f(4)=6,\ f(6)=9,\ f(9)=14,$$
$$f(14)=22,\ f(22)=35,\ f(35)=56,\ f(56)=90,$$
$$f(91)=147,\ f(148)=239,\ f(240)=388.$$
Thus the required value is $388$.''',['Sequences','Functional equations','Counting','Recurrences'])
add(1978,4,'Geometry',5,'Identify the internal tangency point on the symmetry axis; a right-angle cyclic quadrilateral transfers the small circle’s tangent angle into an angle bisector of the original triangle.',r'Let $AB=AC$. A circle touches the circumcircle of $ABC$ internally and touches segments $AB,AC$ at $P,Q$. Prove that the midpoint of $PQ$ is the incenter of $ABC$.',r'''Let $M$ be the midpoint of $PQ$, and let the bisector of $\angle A$ meet the circumcircle again at $R$. Symmetry places the small circle's centre on $AR$; its internal tangency point is $R$ (it cannot be $A$, which lies outside the small circle). Also $M\in AR$ and $PQ\perp AR$.

Since $AR$ is a diameter of the circumcircle, $\angle PBR=90^\circ$. Since $PM\perp MR$, the points $P,B,R,M$ are cyclic. The tangent-chord theorem for the small circle gives $\angle PRQ=\angle APQ=\angle ABC$. Symmetry about $AR$ bisects $\angle PRQ$, so
$$\angle PBM=\angle PRM=\tfrac12\angle ABC.$$
Thus $BM$ bisects $\angle B$, while $AM$ already bisects $\angle A$. Their intersection $M$ is the incenter.''',['Circles','Angles','Cyclic quadrilaterals','Triangle centres'])
add(1978,5,'Algebra',3,'Rearrangement sorts the distinct integers increasingly, and the kth smallest positive integer is at least k; this standard direct inequality is below the intended band.',r'For distinct positive integers $a_1,a_2,\ldots$, prove $\sum_{k=1}^n a_k/k^2\ge\sum_{k=1}^n1/k$ for every $n\ge1$.',r'''Fix $n$ and sort the first $n$ values increasingly as $b_1<\cdots<b_n$. Because the weights $1/k^2$ decrease, exchanging any inverted pair of values cannot increase the weighted sum. Therefore $\sum a_k/k^2\ge\sum b_k/k^2$. Distinct positivity implies $b_k\ge k$, so the latter is at least $\sum k/k^2=\sum1/k$.''',['Rearrangement','Inequalities'])
add(1978,6,'Combinatorics',6.5,'Iterate pigeonhole on difference sets while retaining the fact that new differences occur between elements of every earlier monochromatic set; all six colours are eventually exhausted.',r'Colour the integers $1,\ldots,1978$ with six colours. Prove that some same-coloured integers $x,y,z$ satisfy $x+y=z$, allowing $x=y$.',r'''Suppose no such triple exists. Then a difference of two distinct members of a monochromatic set cannot have that set's colour.

At least 330 integers have one colour. Subtract the least from the others, obtaining 329 positive differences in the other five colours. Choose 66 of one colour, and again subtract the least, obtaining 65 differences in only four colours. Indeed, these new differences are differences of elements of both preceding monochromatic sets, so both preceding colours are excluded.

Repeat. Pigeonhole supplies monochromatic sets of sizes $17$, then $6$, then $3$, then $2$: the corresponding difference-set sizes are $16,5,2$. At each stage the current set is a translate of a subset of every earlier monochromatic set, so differences exclude every colour used so far. The difference of the last two integers therefore has none of the six colours, a contradiction. All differences lie between 1 and 1978. Thus the required triple exists.''',['Pigeonhole principle','Colouring','Ramsey','Difference sets'])
save()
